    <https://schema.org/about> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/dataset/wikimedia-commons-drosophila-compound-eye>.
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch07-04> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasEvidence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/cell-geometry-thompson1917-ch07-04>.
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/cell-geometry-thompson1917-ch07-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/EvidenceItem>;
    <https://schema.org/value> 4;
    <https://schema.org/unitText> "tissues (of those usable) whose junction-angle SD and T-junction fraction are both below the random-Voronoi control, as measured on Cellpose-SAM output (see revisions for segmenter bias)";
    dc:description "Exploratory test of thompson1917-ch07-04: If tissue cell walls meet three at a time at 120 degrees regardless of cell size, junction angles in real tissues cluster near 120 degrees more tightly than in a random partition of the plane, T-shaped junctions are rare, four cells do not meet at one point, and angle deviation does not depend on cell size.\n\nResult: 4 (tissues (of those usable) whose junction-angle SD and T-junction fraction are both below the random-Voronoi control, as measured on Cellpose-SAM output (see revisions for segmenter bias); n = 5; one image per tissue).\n\nCriterion: Exploratory, not pre-registered: the images were segmented and inspected before the pass criterion was written. Treat the verdict as hypothesis-generating; a pre-registered replication on new images is needed before it counts as a test. A tissue conforms if its junction-angle SD and its fraction of T-like junctions (largest angle >= 160 deg) are both below the random-Voronoi control. Supports if every usable tissue conforms; mixed if some do and some do not; refutes if none do.\n\nInterpretation: Thompson's 'tissue cell walls' are taken as segmented cell outlines in 2D images of epithelia and epidermes; the angle is the angle between wall directions near the junction. The compound eye is included as a packing of ommatidia, not of single cells.";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Segmenter bias (added 2026-10-31 after external review): Cellpose-SAM pulls non-hexagonal packings toward 120-degree junctions (brick wall 100% -> 3-16% T-junctions). Conforming tissues are consistent with 120-degree packing but their margin over the random control is inflated; see revisions[0].\nExploratory, not pre-registered: the images were segmented and inspected before the pass criterion was written. Treat the verdict as hypothesis-generating; a pre-registered replication on new images is needed before it counts as a test.\nOne image per tissue, all from Wikimedia Commons; tissues were chosen by availability, not sampled.\nFour-way junction fractions are upper bounds: the random-Voronoi control, which has no true four-way vertices, scores 8.6% because close three-way junctions merge.\nWeak negative size-deviation correlations in some tissues also appear in the random control, so they are attributed to measurement noise in small cells.\nThe Drosophila eye image is an SEM of a curved surface; projection distorts its angles.";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Exploratory cell-geometry test of thompson1917-ch07-04 on 5 images: mixed";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://github.com/fractastical/delphisci/blob/0d6f11c2931e2a6e86516c68a8e05ad599c76355/test-data/claim-tests/thompson-growth-form/thompson1917-ch07-04/result.json>;
    prov:used <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/dataset/wikimedia-commons-drosophila-compound-eye>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/dataset/wikimedia-commons-physcomitrella-leaf>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/dataset/wikimedia-commons-festuca-leaf-epidermis>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/dataset/wikimedia-commons-aloe-leaf-epidermis>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/dataset/wikimedia-commons-human-corneal-endothelium>;
    <https://schema.org/additionalProperty> sub:value0, sub:value1, sub:value10, sub:value11, sub:value12, sub:value13, sub:value14, sub:value15, sub:value16, sub:value17, sub:value18, sub:value19, sub:value2, sub:value20, sub:value21, sub:value22, sub:value23, sub:value24, sub:value25, sub:value26, sub:value27, sub:value3, sub:value4, sub:value5, sub:value6, sub:value7, sub:value8, sub:value9
}
